## BLOCKING
B1 (class, 6 instances). Brief-mandated invariants are not pinned by any
   assertion, so the suite stays green when they are broken.
   The worst instance is M7. Every chain in the real pre-edit handoff uses
   `→`. With the `→` alternative removed, the pre-edit handoff fires only on
   its two headings, all 6 chain hits pass, and a handoff line
   "Order: W-041 → W-039 → W-038." passes (exit 0, page written). The suite
   stays green because [7]'s fixture uses ASCII `->` and [8] only asserts
   absence. The brief calls the guard "the whole point of this opus".
   Instances:
   (a) `→` chain alternative (M7)
   (b) `New opera…` heading alternative (M6; [7] only fixtures `### Backlog`)
   (c) D-021 In-flight caption, which the brief says must be verbatim (M2)
   (d) revisit-trigger footnote (M4)
   (e) ranking-acta link (M5)
   (f) [7]'s "--out is not created OR MODIFIED": only "not created" is
       asserted. No fixture has a pre-existing --out.
   searched: grep of the test file for D-021 / "State as recorded" /
   "earned its opus" / ranking / "New opera" / → / mock-caption / footnote
   (all 0 hits), plus mutations M2 and M4–M7. The survivors are exactly
   (a)–(e). (f) comes from reading [7].
   Remedy:
   - [7]: add a `→` chain fixture (real pre-edit line 122's wrap shape is
     ideal), a `### New opera opened, none started` fixture, and a
     pre-existing --out whose bytes must survive.
   - [1] or a new assertion: caption text, footnote text, and the ranking
     link's href/id.
   - Record reds for the behaviours whose assertions change.
B2 (instance). The `blocked_on: none` sentinel renders as the literal "none",
   not the em dash.
   `bisellium handoff` writes blocked_on "none" when --blocked-on is omitted
   (packages/commands/src/writes.ts:347). blockedOnCell only em-dashes
   null/undefined/empty, so every handed-off non-halted opus shows "none". A
   fresh run at bc53139 shows W-041 as "none". With an open petitio it would
   read "none; P-0xx (needs_you)", which is self-contradictory. The brief's
   rule: "If there is neither, the cell is an em dash."
   Instance, not class. searched: every blocked_on reader (hooks.ts:314,
   query.ts:95, context.ts:146). They print key=value, where "none" reads
   correctly. The renderer is the only one that merges it with other
   blockers.
   Remedy: treat the handoff default "none" as absent in blockedOnCell. Add a
   [2] fixture with blocked_on: none, with and without an open petitio.
B3 (class, 3 instances). The handoff still carries hand-written "next" claims
   naming done opera. This is the Intent's rot #1, surviving.
   (a) "Resume order: 1. W-034 defect 1 … full pipeline". W-034 is done.
   (b) "Resume point … 1. W-033 … is next per the standing ranking. W-044 …
       candidate to jump the queue". W-033 and W-044 are both done.
   (c) Builder-added "…ranking acta; it starts at W-041". This is a new
       hand-written ordering fact outside an acta, and it goes stale the
       moment W-041 merges.
   The brief keeps only "mode, throttle and checkpoint notes" around the
   replaced chains. These three are slate statements, not those. Acceptance 4
   passes literally, but the file still disagrees with itself about what is
   first, which is exactly the Intent's first bullet.
   searched: the full post-edit "Resume order" and "Resume point" blocks,
   plus grep of every W-0xx id in the handoff's edited hunks against its
   state: field.
   Remedy: delete (a), (b)'s item 1 and (c), and leave the links. SESSION-HANDOFF.md is an owned file.

## ADVISORY (non-blocking)
A1 esc() does not escape `"`, but it feeds attributes (data-id, the ranking
   href). Ids are CLI-validated and the href is built from a filename, so the
   risk is low. One .replace(/"/g,"&quot;") closes it.
A2 An opus whose state is outside the seven lifecycle ids is dropped
   silently (no row, no error). check validates states, so this is low
   priority.
A3 The post-merge checkpoint must run build.sh (claim-6 ruling). Otherwise
   trunk lists W-041 (done) as building.

